  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nToday-Sunday... An upper level cut-off low will move westward across \nthe Florida peninsula today before stalling over the eastern Gulf \nSunday. At the surface, broad high pressure will remain across the \nFlorida peninsula through the weekend. Locally, deep moisture will \nincrease, with forecast PW values of 1.8-2.0\" both days. East to \nnortheast winds will persist through the rest of the weekend, with \nspeeds generally 5-10 mph. Near normal to slightly higher than \nnormal rain chances are forecast each day. There is a medium (30-60 \npercent) chance of rain today, and a medium to high (40-70 percent) \nchance on Sunday. Greatest coverage of showers and storms will \ngenerally be across the interior (west of I-95) today and across the \nsouth, mainly from Kissimmee to Melbourne southward, on Sunday. \nIsolated to scattered onshore-moving showers are forecast in the \nmorning before coverage increases and transitions over land in the \nafternoon as daytime heating occurs and the east coast sea breeze \nforms and pushes inland each day. The environment supports \nconvection with sufficient instability (1500-1700 J/kg of MUCAPE), \nadequate shear (around 20 KT of sfc-8km), cooler temperatures aloft \n(-7C to -8C at 500 mb), and sufficient downdraft potential (600-800 \nJ/kg). Because of this, the main storm hazards will be frequent \nlightning strikes, gusty wind of 45-50 mph, small hail, and locally \nheavy rainfall of 1-3 inches each day. Any lingering shower and \nstorm activity will diminish each evening, with mostly dry \nconditions expected overnight. However, isolated to scattered \nshowers and storms will continue to be possible over the Atlantic \nwaters overnight and into the morning, with some of this activity \npotentially moving onshore. \n\nSeasonable temperatures are forecast through the rest of this \nweekend. Afternoon highs will be in the upper 80s to low 90s with \npeak heat indices of 100-107 degrees. Warm and muggy conditions \ncontinue each night, with overnight lows in the low to mid 70s. \nThere is a moderate risk of rip currents at all area beaches this \nweekend. Always swim near a lifeguard and never swim alone! \n\nMonday- Friday... The upper level low off the west coast of Florida \nto start off the week will slowly lift northeast before becoming \nabsorbed by a northern stream trough early week. The trough will \nslowly shift eastward towards the eastern coast, eventually moving \noffshore the eastern US by mid-late week. At the surface, a frontal \nboundary is forecast to reach central Florida Thursday before \nstalling across south Florida on Friday. Locally, deep moisture will \nremain through mid week, supporting high rain and storm chances, \nwith a high (60-80 percent) chance of rain Monday-Wednesday. \nSlightly drier air will filter across the local area into late week, \nresulting in rain chances becoming near normal, with a medium to \nhigh (40-70 percent) chance of rain Thursday-Friday. Main storm \nhazards will continue to be frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds \naround 45 mph, and locally heavy rainfall. Seasonable temperatures \nare forecast through the period, with highs in  the upper 80s to low \n90s. These temperatures combined with humidity will produce peak \nheat indices of 95-105 degrees each day. Warm and muggy conditions \nforecast each night, with overnight lows in the low to mid 70s.",
